i think the microvax-2 runs at 20 MHz as it is, theres a 40 MHz crystal on the
ka630.  i dont know if it divides the clock or has wait states for memory
(given the speed of the memory, i's assume it does, though), but if one could
porpely divide everything else out (the qbus interface would have to be
capable of running at its proper speed and still working with regard to the
78032) , i can imagine with special cooling maybe doing 100 or even 150 MHz in
there. That'd be really special cooling.

I just found some massive reference of cpus somewhere from google,
at http://members.tripod.com/kmi9000/kmi_proc.htm, that has this to say
about the 78032:

       MicroVAX-32 (78032=DC333 CPU + 78132=DC337 FPU, SID=0x08, ZMOS
       (4-type NMOS) 3.0 micron, 101k trans., 40 MHz master clock for
       8-phase 200 nsec microcycle, used in MicroVAX II and VAXstation
       2000 and VAX 8200, 1985)

ah, so internally its only running ar 5 mhz really.. Still, with 3 micron
process, clocking it up that high will dissipate ridiculous energy.. the board
uses 30 watts or so as it is..

On the netbsd port-vax list there has come up a couple of times the
idea of doing an 11/750 in fpga's.. Several people on the list, myself included,
