given that a 78032 is only 101000 transistors, if it were shrunk to
modern process, i can see it easily running at a couple gighertz.
Given the size, the thing would only be on the order of 1mm square,
Add a bigger L1 cache on it and you would have probably 60 VUPS in a package
smaller than a 386. An even cooler idea might be to put, on a die
still smaller than a modern cpu, 4 microvax cpus, a generous cache, and
a decent (hypertransport?) interface.  the microvax-2, though, is a very
simple design.. many of the modern tricks are absent from it.. the nvax
was a much more clever machine, and quite fast.. a shrink of NVAX to
modern process would make it very cheap (1.3 million transistors), fit in
a 2x3 mm die, fill it out to a 9x9 mm die with a meg of cache and a
hypertransport interface, and you have a competetive, modern processor.
sell it as the gigavax. of course, nowadays the biggest liability would
be the tiny address space.. 2 gigabytes of physical core is small for
serious database servers now.. though such a machine would
certainly make for a line of pizza box vax workstations which could
keep anybody who still runs a vax in business for another decade (seriously,
people still run businesses on vaxstation 3100s sometimes!!)

Isildur

