I'm experimenting with some old hardware which I think was OK when I got 
it but which I have never properly brought up.

One piece is a 4000-100A.  It apparently has a DSSI controller as well
as a SCSI controller.  There is a DSSI disk and a SCSI CD inside.  It
has a HUGE connection block on the back with a 50-pin
Centronics---presumably SCSI---as well as male and female connectors
with three rows of pins (otherwise about the same size as the Centronics
connector) and three male plugs which look like the small 50 (or 68?)
pin SCSI connectors but almost certainly aren't.  It came with a BA350
with a cable connecting it to one of the small plugs.  In the BA350
there is some sort of module which I suspect is a DSSI-SCSI converter
(since almost certainly only SCSI disks can go into the BA 350). 

Anyway, the default boot device is non-existent.  When I tell it to boot 
from the DSSI disk inside, it comes up saying VMS 5.5-2H4, asks for the 
date and time and then says it has booted standalone backup!  I then 
have a "$" prompt, but it's not a DCL prompt.  Probably it is the 
standalone-backup prompt, since BACKUP seems to be the only command it 
recognises.

I'm not very familiar with standalone backup.  Does anyone know if there
is anyway to tell whether the DSSI disk inside is bootable and if so how
to boot from it (from the console prompt) rather than have it boot into
standalone backup? 

Another piece is a 4000-90.  At power up, the four LEDs on the left
(1--4) stay lit all the time.  At first, the leftmost two in the group 
on the right (5--6) stay lit, then 5 stays on while 6 goes out, 7 comes 
up and stays lit and 8 flashes very rapidly.  Any idea what this means?

Another piece is a 3100-38.  It appears to have a SCSI connector on the 
back, but a broad, narrow one (talking about the physical dimensions 
here) with small pins.  Will this work with DEC SCSI disks from the same 
error (keeping in mind the 1.07 GB limitation for a boot disk) if I have 
the appropriate connector?



