Phillip Helbig wrote:
> One piece is a 4000-100A.  It apparently has a DSSI controller as well
> as a SCSI controller.  There is a DSSI disk and a SCSI CD inside.  It
> has a HUGE connection block on the back with a 50-pin
> Centronics---presumably SCSI---as well as male and female connectors

The VAX 4000-100A and the MicroVAX 3100-90 (IIRC) are the same
mainboard but in different cases; the mainboard in the 100A has
been told (via the commadn that tells it its id) that it should
activate the DSSI and Q-bus support. There is no internal Q-bus,
instead you get to connect via a particular extender cable to
an external Q-bus cab. One of those external connectors is
the Q-bus expansion connector. I've never played with a
4000-100A but I suspect that the big connector is it.
If you cannot find the owner's manual, let me know and
I'll track it down; I'm sure it's on the web somewhere.

> Anyway, the default boot device is non-existent.  When I tell it to boot 
> from the DSSI disk inside, it comes up saying VMS 5.5-2H4, asks for the 
> date and time and then says it has booted standalone backup!  I then 
> have a "$" prompt, but it's not a DCL prompt.  Probably it is the 
> standalone-backup prompt, since BACKUP seems to be the only command it 
> recognises.
> 
> I'm not very familiar with standalone backup.  Does anyone know if there
> is anyway to tell whether the DSSI disk inside is bootable and if so how
> to boot from it (from the console prompt) rather than have it boot into
> standalone backup? 

Well if you've booted SABKP, then you've definitely booted off
a bootable device. Since you told it to boot from a DSSI disk
and you ended up in SABKP it seems reasonable to conclude that
the internal DSSI disk is indeed bootable!

When SABKP comes up it should list all the devices it
knows about. Other than either restoring, creating or
listing a backup saveset, it cannot do much.

You could see if the console variable BFLAGS (I think that's
the one ... try HELP SET and see) has been set. But it would
be unusual to have a machine set to default boot into anything
other than a fully-fledged OS.


> Another piece is a 4000-90.  At power up, the four LEDs on the left
> (1--4) stay lit all the time.  At first, the leftmost two in the group 
> on the right (5--6) stay lit, then 5 stays on while 6 goes out, 7 comes 
> up and stays lit and 8 flashes very rapidly.  Any idea what this means?

That one is not listed in the Owner's manual. I have the service guide 
somewhere, but not to hand. You might try hooking up a VT terminal to
the printer port on the read (I think either the DB25 or the MMJ
will work - my recollection is that they are the same interface with
two styles of external port) and flick up S3 at the front (under the 
flap). That might give you more info.

> Another piece is a 3100-38.  It appears to have a SCSI connector on the 
> back, but a broad, narrow one (talking about the physical dimensions 
> here) with small pins.  Will this work with DEC SCSI disks from the same 
> error (keeping in mind the 1.07 GB limitation for a boot disk) if I have 
> the appropriate connector?

I've had much more modern disks work in those (assuming a 1997-era
1GB IBM SCSI disk counts as "modern").

Antonio

