In article <ba8a9l$sr3$1@online.de>, helbig@astro.multiCLOTHESvax.de (Phillip Helbig---remove CLOTHES to reply) writes:
> 
> One piece is a 4000-100A.  It apparently has a DSSI controller as well
> as a SCSI controller.  

> There is a DSSI disk and a SCSI CD inside.  It
> has a HUGE connection block on the back with a 50-pin
> Centronics---presumably SCSI---as well as male and female connectors
> with three rows of pins (otherwise about the same size as the Centronics
> connector) 

   That would be Qbus.  It takes two cables to connect the 4000-100A to
   a Qbus chassis.  You're lucky to have yours on the back, mine has
   them on the top, in a rectangular bump.

> and three male plugs which look like the small 50 (or 68?)
> pin SCSI connectors but almost certainly aren't.

   If there's a post on each side, those are the DSSI connectors.

>  It came with a BA350
> with a cable connecting it to one of the small plugs.  In the BA350
> there is some sort of module which I suspect is a DSSI-SCSI converter
> (since almost certainly only SCSI disks can go into the BA 350). 

   HSJ style DSSI-SCSI converter, which lets one put an entire SCSI bus
   on a single DSSI node.  Very popular with BA350 chassis and DSSI-only
   boxes, but for this one I would have plugged the native SCSI into the
   BA350.

> 
> Anyway, the default boot device is non-existent.  When I tell it to boot 
> from the DSSI disk inside, it comes up saying VMS 5.5-2H4, asks for the 
> date and time and then says it has booted standalone backup!  I then 
> have a "$" prompt, but it's not a DCL prompt.  Probably it is the 
> standalone-backup prompt, since BACKUP seems to be the only command it 
> recognises.
> 
> I'm not very familiar with standalone backup.  Does anyone know if there
> is anyway to tell whether the DSSI disk inside is bootable and if so how
> to boot from it (from the console prompt) rather than have it boot into
> standalone backup? 

   If b/0 boots standalone backup, then that's all that's on that disk.
   If both VMS and standalone backup were on there, you'ld normally use
   b/e0000000 to get to standalone backup.  Check boot_flags or
   something similar at the console prompt to see if e0000000 is the
   default.


