"Dr. David Kirkby" <davek@medphys.ucl.ac.uk> writes in article <3EAFCFE6.8D8DF41A@medphys.ucl.ac.uk> dated Wed, 30 Apr 2003 14:30:14 +0100:
>I have a Dec Personal Workstation 600a, on which I have just about
>installed Tru64 UNIX - I say just about, as I seem to have an issue with
>passwords, but the hardware seems okay. Currently the machine has a 4.5
>Gb SCSI hard disk and an IDE CD-ROM.
>
>I was thinking of putting a larger disk in the machine. Given the costs
>of SCSI vs IDE, I was thinking of using an IDE disk, rather than a SCSI
>one. Does anyone know if there are any limitations on the size of IDE
>disks that can be used, either due to limits on Tru64 (latest version)
>of the 600a's hardware? I don't want to buy 120 Gb and find I can only
>use 8 Gb!!

I have a 500au running VMS.  IDE hard drives are "unsupported", but I stuck
a 60 GB in anyway, and it works.  However, using the IDE drive really eats
up the CPU time.  I wouldn't use the PWS's IDE interface for a boot or swap
disk, but it's OK for data as long as you don't need super-speed.  

Tru64 is probably the same.  

I have heard you can get a SCSI-IDE bridge that allows you to use IDE drives
through your SCSI interface.  If you want performance, that's one option.  

