Not that I would actually do this, but...

I wouldn't be too sure about your pricing considering that you can get
a 7200 RPM IDE drive that will hold 80GB for about $80 just about anywhere
(and a few dollars less if you shop around for the best price), and possibly
a 120GB drive for not too much over that $80 if you shop around (I saw one
that, including a mail-in rebate, would have been something like $82 about a
week ago). And as you go for bigger drives, the differential gets worse (for
the SCSI) - a 200 GB IDE drive runs about $200, i.e. the pricing is nearly
linear: the best prices are slightly under a dollar per GB down around 80GB,
right about at a dollar per GB at 200GB, and a little over a dollar per GB
at the 250GB point. For $80 you might be able to get a SCSI drive somewhere
around 60GB in size. For $200 you might be able to get one a bit over 80GB.
If you want one around 200 GB it'll probably cost you somewhat over $850.
Unlike the IDE drives, the SCSI drive pricing is not much like linear with
size. The converter apparently runs about $99 per drive. So for $850 you
might be able to buy one 200 GB SCSI drive, or you could buy two 250GB IDE
drives and two converters and have at least $100 left over. 200 GB or both
500 GB and money left over, take your pick.

That IDE drive is not quite as fast as a 10000 RPM SCSI drive of similar
size, and may even be slightly slower than a 7200 RPM SCSI drive of similar
size, but it costs a lot less at the higher capacities.

It is a somewhat attractive option if you need as much storage as you can
possibly fit into your case and don't want to pay SCSI drive prices. (And
don't need the fastest drives you can get your hands on.)

Reliability may suffer some too, though (which is why I wouldn't do this).
Backups are your friend.

--- Carl


