	My boss mentioned at our group meeting today (and we're involved
with *lots* of data-intensive projects and many terabytes of disk space)
that modern IDEs seem to have overstepped the reliability limit, and
especially that a common RAID converter that attaches IDE drives to a SCSI
bus (if I'm remembering right) was invariably throwing up errors
eventually. His recommendation for IDE drives was a 200-hour continual
re-write burn-in before commissioning, due to a very high infant mortality
rate in recent drives.  (He pointed out that IDE drive manufacturers now
typically give a 1-year warranty, while most SCSI drives have a 5-year
warranty.)

